WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.850
MIKE COLEMAN: Hi I'm Mike
Coleman, a developer advocate

00:00:02.850 --> 00:00:04.230
here at Google Cloud.

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:05.760
In this video, I
want to walk you

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:08.580
through how you can use Google
Cloud's Migrate for Anthos

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:10.920
to move some of your
existing Windows workloads

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:14.610
from virtual machines to
containers in just a few steps.

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:16.379
This video builds
on an earlier one,

00:00:16.379 --> 00:00:18.170
introducing Migrate for Anthos.

00:00:18.170 --> 00:00:19.920
If you haven't checked
that video out yet,

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:22.350
definitely go do so,
as this one assumes

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:25.248
you performed the prerequisites
we outlined there.

00:00:25.248 --> 00:00:27.540
At the end of the last video,
we had set up our service

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:29.580
accounts, enabled
the necessary APIs,

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:31.560
and created a
processing cluster.

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:33.480
Before we can do the
actual migration,

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:37.590
we need to complete a couple of
Windows-specific prerequisites.

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:40.620
We need to add a Windows node
pool to our processing cluster,

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:43.470
and ensure we have
a Windows Build VM.

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:45.510
We need the node pool
to ensure that we can

00:00:45.510 --> 00:00:47.130
analyze Windows applications.

00:00:47.130 --> 00:00:49.320
And unlike with
Linux applications,

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:51.210
there isn't an
automated container

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:53.250
build step with Windows apps.

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:55.530
Migrate for Anthos will
create a Docker file,

00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:57.210
but you will need
a VM on which you

00:00:57.210 --> 00:00:59.520
can do the actual
Docker build as well

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:02.280
as push the resulting image
up to the Google Container

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:03.360
Registry.

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:05.250
So let's set both of those up.

00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:07.052
I've logged into the
Google Cloud console,

00:01:07.052 --> 00:01:08.260
and I'm ready to get started.

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:10.200
So I'll click on
Processing Cluster.

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:12.130
I'll go ahead and
add the node pool.

00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:14.170
We're going to go with one node.

00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:15.800
So let's click that there.

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:17.550
Come over here, we're
going to change this

00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:20.220
to the Windows semiannual
channel release,

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:21.732
so we'll click Make Changes.

00:01:21.732 --> 00:01:23.190
I want to come down
here and I want

00:01:23.190 --> 00:01:28.830
to use an N 1 standard 4 as
my machine for the node pool,

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:31.020
and we'll come down,
and then for security, I

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:34.410
want to make sure that I
have full platform access,

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:37.410
and we'll click that, and
then we'll click Create.

00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:38.790
And that will add the node pool.

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:40.290
So next thing I
want to do is I want

00:01:40.290 --> 00:01:43.900
to add the machine that I'll
use to build my Docker images.

00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:45.400
So we're going to
come up over here,

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:48.420
and we're going to click on
Compute Engine, Virtual Machine

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:53.270
Instances, we'll
do Create Instance.

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:55.700
We'll call the
machine Win Build 1.

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:58.490
We're going to put it in
the US West 2A region,

00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:00.680
we're going to use
an N 1 standard 4,

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:04.310
and we'll use Windows Server
1909 Data Center Edition.

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:06.740
We want to come down here and
ensure we have full platform

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:08.900
scope again, and
we'll click Create,

00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:11.870
and that will go off and
create our build machine.

00:02:11.870 --> 00:02:14.160
With those additional
prerequisites set up,

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:16.220
let's jump into using
Migrate for Anthos

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:18.440
to migrate a
Windows application.

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:20.690
As a brief recap,
Migrate for Anthos

00:02:20.690 --> 00:02:22.947
has three primary components.

00:02:22.947 --> 00:02:24.530
The first is the
discovery tool, which

00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:26.930
assesses how successful
a migration of a given

00:02:26.930 --> 00:02:28.460
application might be.

00:02:28.460 --> 00:02:31.550
Next is the processing
cluster, a kubernetes cluster

00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:33.470
that is used to
examine the source VM

00:02:33.470 --> 00:02:36.320
and create the migration
plan, as well as execute

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:37.940
the actual migration.

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:41.180
And finally, migctl,
a command line tool,

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:45.260
like kube ctl, for interacting
with the processing cluster.

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:47.540
We'll do most of our work
in the console today,

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:50.300
but I will show you
a bit of migctl.

00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:52.550
And again, to quickly
recap, a migration

00:02:52.550 --> 00:02:55.250
generally consists of
the following steps.

00:02:55.250 --> 00:02:58.490
Creating a Google Cloud project,
setting up service accounts,

00:02:58.490 --> 00:03:01.340
and enabling the
appropriate APIs,

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:04.100
creating migration processing
cluster and a build

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:07.580
VM in the case of Windows,
choosing and qualifying

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:11.660
your workload, defining
your migration source,

00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:15.320
creating a migration plan, and
adjusting the migration plan

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:18.380
to ensure it meets your
needs, creating the deployment

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:21.350
artifacts, and deploying
the newly containerised

00:03:21.350 --> 00:03:22.650
application.

00:03:22.650 --> 00:03:24.660
We now have the first two items.

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:28.280
So let's jump into how you can
choose the right application.

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:30.290
The first thing to
consider is which

00:03:30.290 --> 00:03:35.030
operating systems IIS and dot
NET versions can be migrated.

00:03:35.030 --> 00:03:37.700
We migrate Microsoft
Windows Server 2008

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:42.470
R2 or higher, Microsoft IIS
7, or higher web applications,

00:03:42.470 --> 00:03:45.260
and ASP.NET and dot
NET framework versions

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:47.120
3.5 or higher.

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:49.310
It's important to note
that after Migration,

00:03:49.310 --> 00:03:54.320
the resulting container will
run Windows Server version 1909.

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:57.440
In addition to specific versions
of the operating system or app

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:00.140
stack, the following
application characteristics

00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:02.330
make them unsuitable
for migration.

00:04:02.330 --> 00:04:06.080
Applications with
dependencies on GPUs or TPUs,

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:09.350
applications with low level
networking requirements,

00:04:09.350 --> 00:04:12.890
desktop, RDP, and
VDI applications,

00:04:12.890 --> 00:04:15.590
and applications with
bring your own licensing.

00:04:15.590 --> 00:04:18.260
In order to help determine if
an application is a good fit,

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:20.899
we have the Application
Discovery Tool.

00:04:20.899 --> 00:04:23.810
The tool outputs a zip file
that indicates whether or not

00:04:23.810 --> 00:04:26.630
the Windows VM is a
potential fit for Migration.

00:04:26.630 --> 00:04:29.270
The name of the file
indicates the results.

00:04:29.270 --> 00:04:31.220
The zip file includes
multiple files

00:04:31.220 --> 00:04:35.225
with information on the
operating system settings, IIS,

00:04:35.225 --> 00:04:38.180
odbc config, and the
global assembly cache.

00:04:38.180 --> 00:04:40.880
I'm not going to go into the
zip file contents in depth,

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:42.800
and would encourage you
to check out the docs

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:44.867
if you need more information.

00:04:44.867 --> 00:04:46.950
Let's take a look at the
Migration tool in action.

00:04:46.950 --> 00:04:48.658
But before we do that,
I want to give you

00:04:48.658 --> 00:04:51.590
an overview of the application
we'll be migrating.

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:54.200
This is a simple
ASP.NET application.

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:55.700
It's got this
webfront end running

00:04:55.700 --> 00:04:58.770
on IIS that connects back
to a Cloud SQL instance.

00:04:58.770 --> 00:05:00.898
So if I click on
Courses, you can

00:05:00.898 --> 00:05:02.190
see a bunch of courses come up.

00:05:02.190 --> 00:05:05.060
Those are being pulled out
of the Cloud SQL database.

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:06.502
The virtual machine
is right here.

00:05:06.502 --> 00:05:08.210
It's called Cymbal
University, and that's

00:05:08.210 --> 00:05:09.860
what we're going to migrate.

00:05:09.860 --> 00:05:13.540
I've already set up an
RDP session into that box,

00:05:13.540 --> 00:05:15.730
so we'll move into
that now, and I've

00:05:15.730 --> 00:05:18.490
installed the Migrate for
Anthos migration tool.

00:05:18.490 --> 00:05:20.620
You can see it right here,
collect info dot exe.

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:23.650
So I'll hit Enter, and
we'll run that tool.

00:05:23.650 --> 00:05:25.150
It's going to go
through, it's going

00:05:25.150 --> 00:05:26.860
to collect a bunch of
system information.

00:05:26.860 --> 00:05:28.050
That's going to
take a little bit,

00:05:28.050 --> 00:05:29.675
so I'm going to fast
forward the video,

00:05:29.675 --> 00:05:31.960
and I'll rejoin you in a second.

00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:36.810
So the tool is finished, and if
we look at the file name here,

00:05:36.810 --> 00:05:39.540
it doesn't say no
fit, which means

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:41.020
we actually do have a fit.

00:05:41.020 --> 00:05:42.930
So our application
is good to go,

00:05:42.930 --> 00:05:44.880
and should be an easy migration.

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:46.620
We have completed
our prerequisites,

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:48.413
and our application
is a good fit.

00:05:48.413 --> 00:05:50.580
So at this point, we're
ready to go ahead and create

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:52.200
our migration source.

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:54.270
Before we start creating
actual migration,

00:05:54.270 --> 00:05:56.230
I just wanted to review
a couple of points.

00:05:56.230 --> 00:05:58.890
First, a Windows
instance can only

00:05:58.890 --> 00:06:01.565
be migrated from a
Compute Engine instance.

00:06:01.565 --> 00:06:03.190
So that's what we've
got going on here.

00:06:03.190 --> 00:06:05.850
We've got Cymbal University
on a Compute Engine VM.

00:06:05.850 --> 00:06:08.873
The second thing is, you cannot
migrate a VM until you stop it.

00:06:08.873 --> 00:06:11.040
So I'm going to come over
here to Cymbal University,

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:14.770
I'm going to select it, and
I'm going to click Stop.

00:06:14.770 --> 00:06:16.720
With the VM stopped,
I can now go

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:19.120
to Migrate to Containers
here in the menu,

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:21.610
and start the migration process.

00:06:21.610 --> 00:06:24.320
We added our processing
cluster earlier, so here it is.

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:25.680
It's in the UI.

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:27.430
Now we're going to add
a migration source.

00:06:27.430 --> 00:06:30.390
So I'm going to
click Add Source,

00:06:30.390 --> 00:06:33.390
I'm going to select that
processing cluster, click Next,

00:06:33.390 --> 00:06:34.140
give it a name.

00:06:34.140 --> 00:06:36.127
Windows Migration Source.

00:06:36.127 --> 00:06:37.710
It's going to come
off Compute Engine.

00:06:37.710 --> 00:06:38.518
Click Next.

00:06:38.518 --> 00:06:40.560
And now, we're going to
create a service account.

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:43.120
So we can have this done
automatically for us.

00:06:43.120 --> 00:06:45.750
The service account is going to
have the viewer and the storage

00:06:45.750 --> 00:06:47.110
admin roles assigned to it.

00:06:47.110 --> 00:06:49.620
So we'll go ahead
and click Next.

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:53.070
And Add Source, and that will
add our migration source.

00:06:55.790 --> 00:06:56.990
The source is now ready.

00:06:56.990 --> 00:06:58.910
It can handle Windows
and Linux workloads.

00:06:58.910 --> 00:07:01.010
So let's create the
actual migration.

00:07:01.010 --> 00:07:03.020
Click Create Migration,
come in here,

00:07:03.020 --> 00:07:06.630
give the migration a name.

00:07:06.630 --> 00:07:08.390
We'll call it Windows Migration.

00:07:08.390 --> 00:07:11.430
Select the source that we just
created from the dropdown,

00:07:11.430 --> 00:07:13.640
select the operating
system as Windows,

00:07:13.640 --> 00:07:16.640
and then type in the name
of the virtual machine

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:19.820
that we're going to migrate,
which was Cymbal University.

00:07:19.820 --> 00:07:23.660
We'll come down here and
we'll click Create Migration.

00:07:23.660 --> 00:07:26.130
That's going to start the
creation of the migration plan,

00:07:26.130 --> 00:07:27.870
and that's going to
take a few seconds.

00:07:27.870 --> 00:07:29.787
So let me go ahead and
fast forward the video,

00:07:29.787 --> 00:07:32.620
and I'll rejoin you
when that's done.

00:07:32.620 --> 00:07:34.810
OK, the migration plan
has been generated.

00:07:34.810 --> 00:07:36.970
And the next step here
would be to review and edit

00:07:36.970 --> 00:07:38.135
that migration plan.

00:07:38.135 --> 00:07:39.760
So we come in and we
take a look at it.

00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:40.990
We're not actually
going to edit it.

00:07:40.990 --> 00:07:42.670
We're not going to spend
a lot of time here.

00:07:42.670 --> 00:07:44.020
I'll just point out
some highlights.

00:07:44.020 --> 00:07:45.853
You can see here we're
running a dot NET 4 5

00:07:45.853 --> 00:07:48.400
classic application,
here's the web

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:51.620
route directory, information
like that, port 80.

00:07:51.620 --> 00:07:53.180
So all of that looks good.

00:07:53.180 --> 00:07:56.477
So let's go ahead and save that
and generate our artifacts.

00:07:56.477 --> 00:07:58.810
Now, this is going to go out
and create our Docker file.

00:07:58.810 --> 00:08:00.560
Again, this is going to
take a couple seconds,

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:02.435
so I'm going to go ahead
and pause the video,

00:08:02.435 --> 00:08:05.260
and I'll rejoin you
when it's finished.

00:08:05.260 --> 00:08:06.880
The artifacts have
been generated,

00:08:06.880 --> 00:08:09.820
and those are actually stored in
a Google Cloud Storage bucket.

00:08:09.820 --> 00:08:12.400
And there's a couple different
ways you could get at them.

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:13.900
You could go to the
bucket directly,

00:08:13.900 --> 00:08:17.560
or you can actually use migctl
to give you the location,

00:08:17.560 --> 00:08:18.933
and then copy the file down.

00:08:18.933 --> 00:08:20.350
And that's what
we're going to do.

00:08:20.350 --> 00:08:22.540
So I'm going to open
up a Cloud Shell,

00:08:22.540 --> 00:08:25.900
and I'm going to issue that
migctl Get Artifacts command,

00:08:25.900 --> 00:08:28.120
and point at the
migration we just did,

00:08:28.120 --> 00:08:30.830
and that's going to give me
the command that I need to run.

00:08:30.830 --> 00:08:34.059
So I'm going to copy
that command, gs util cp,

00:08:34.059 --> 00:08:37.353
gs util copy, and we're going
to run that in our Windows

00:08:37.353 --> 00:08:39.770
build machine where we're going
to build our Docker image.

00:08:39.770 --> 00:08:42.010
So let's run that copy
command, and that's

00:08:42.010 --> 00:08:43.900
going to bring down
the artifacts dot zip

00:08:43.900 --> 00:08:45.550
file, so it's been copied.

00:08:45.550 --> 00:08:48.310
Let's look at the directory
and make sure it's there.

00:08:48.310 --> 00:08:50.530
Clear the screen,
and we'll do a DIR,

00:08:50.530 --> 00:08:52.160
and there's our artifacts file.

00:08:52.160 --> 00:08:56.110
So let's expand that archive
to get to the Docker file.

00:08:56.110 --> 00:08:58.450
So Expand Archive,
artifacts dot zip,

00:08:58.450 --> 00:09:01.240
hit Enter, archive
will get expanded out,

00:09:01.240 --> 00:09:05.480
and we will have
our Docker file.

00:09:05.480 --> 00:09:07.080
All right, let's
clear the screen.

00:09:07.080 --> 00:09:08.730
Let's take a look at
that artifacts directory

00:09:08.730 --> 00:09:10.230
to make sure we got
our Docker file.

00:09:10.230 --> 00:09:12.600
So do the directory, and
there's the Docker file.

00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:15.170
So now let's issue our
Docker build command,

00:09:15.170 --> 00:09:18.590
and tag it so it can be pushed
up to Google Container Registry

00:09:18.590 --> 00:09:21.130
after the build is finished.

00:09:21.130 --> 00:09:22.880
So we'll go ahead and
get that build done.

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:25.370
There it is, it's been tagged
and ready to get pushed up

00:09:25.370 --> 00:09:28.020
to the Container Registry,
so let's do that next.

00:09:28.020 --> 00:09:30.230
So we'll clear the
screen, and we'll

00:09:30.230 --> 00:09:31.890
issue the Docker push command.

00:09:31.890 --> 00:09:34.310
And that will push it up to
Google Container Registry.

00:09:34.310 --> 00:09:35.850
This is going to
take a few seconds,

00:09:35.850 --> 00:09:37.475
so I'm going to fast
forward the video,

00:09:37.475 --> 00:09:38.750
and rejoin you when it's done.

00:09:41.452 --> 00:09:43.410
As that gets pushed to
your Container Registry,

00:09:43.410 --> 00:09:45.810
the next thing to do is
to actually deploy it

00:09:45.810 --> 00:09:47.380
to our production cluster.

00:09:47.380 --> 00:09:50.010
So I've got two clusters,
a processing cluster

00:09:50.010 --> 00:09:51.600
and a production
cluster, and I'm

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:53.100
going to go into
Cloud Shell and I'm

00:09:53.100 --> 00:09:55.830
going to log into my
production cluster.

00:09:55.830 --> 00:09:58.090
And then, to make sure I
am on the right cluster,

00:09:58.090 --> 00:10:00.300
I'm going to do a get
nodes, and make sure

00:10:00.300 --> 00:10:03.700
that I see all the nodes of my
production cluster, which I do.

00:10:03.700 --> 00:10:05.820
So now let's take a look
at our deployment yaml,

00:10:05.820 --> 00:10:07.500
and you can see
nothing special here.

00:10:07.500 --> 00:10:09.330
I'm deploying that
Container Registry image

00:10:09.330 --> 00:10:12.060
that I pushed up earlier, and
so let's go ahead and do that.

00:10:12.060 --> 00:10:15.600
kube ctl apply minus
F, symbol deployment

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:18.900
dot yaml will create my
kubernetes deployment.

00:10:18.900 --> 00:10:20.838
So give that a minute
to settle down,

00:10:20.838 --> 00:10:22.380
and then we'll check
the status here.

00:10:25.090 --> 00:10:26.540
Clear the screen.

00:10:26.540 --> 00:10:28.750
And let's check and see
if our pods are there.

00:10:28.750 --> 00:10:29.770
And there's our pod.

00:10:29.770 --> 00:10:33.240
Check our deployments.

00:10:33.240 --> 00:10:34.410
And the deployment is there.

00:10:34.410 --> 00:10:36.930
Now let's go ahead and
expose that deployment

00:10:36.930 --> 00:10:38.357
behind a load balancer.

00:10:38.357 --> 00:10:39.690
This will take a couple seconds.

00:10:39.690 --> 00:10:41.850
I'm going to pause the
video and I'll rejoin you

00:10:41.850 --> 00:10:44.250
when it's up and running.

00:10:44.250 --> 00:10:47.040
All right, let's do a
kube ctl get services,

00:10:47.040 --> 00:10:50.430
and that will list out the IP
address of our load balancer,

00:10:50.430 --> 00:10:52.800
so we're going to
grab that IP address,

00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:56.040
we're going to come up here
into Chrome and open a new tab,

00:10:56.040 --> 00:11:00.750
paste in that address, click
there, paste the address in,

00:11:00.750 --> 00:11:03.690
and that will open the
Cymbal University website.

00:11:03.690 --> 00:11:04.680
So let's hit Enter.

00:11:04.680 --> 00:11:05.550
There's the website.

00:11:05.550 --> 00:11:06.570
Let's click Courses.

00:11:06.570 --> 00:11:08.190
We're still connected
to our database,

00:11:08.190 --> 00:11:10.470
and if I go back into
Google Cloud platform,

00:11:10.470 --> 00:11:12.090
and I look at the
VM instance, I'll

00:11:12.090 --> 00:11:14.160
see that it's still
stopped, meaning

00:11:14.160 --> 00:11:16.650
that I am in fact serving
my application now

00:11:16.650 --> 00:11:20.295
off of the kubernetes cluster,
out of that container.

00:11:20.295 --> 00:11:21.170
So there you have it.

00:11:21.170 --> 00:11:23.545
In this video, you learned
about using Migrate for Anthos

00:11:23.545 --> 00:11:26.050
